{"kind":"stack","id":"flowers-life-cycle","title":"How flowers make new plants","theme":{"background":"#f4fbf2","foreground":"#1f3d2b","font":"Comic Sans MS, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-term","content":"Pollination"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-def","content":"When pollen moves from the anther of one flower to the stigma of a flower. This can be done by wind, insects or birds."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-next","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-q","content":"What is the job of the stigma in a flower?"},{"type":"button","id":"c2-a","label":"To catch pollen","why":"The stigma is the sticky top part that catches pollen grains.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"To make the seeds","why":"Pupils mix up the stigma with the ovary, which holds the seeds.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-c","label":"To make the petals bright","why":"Pupils think the stigma is the coloured part that attracts insects.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Which part of the flower makes the pollen?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"The anther","why":"The anther is the part at the top of the stamen that makes pollen.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"The stigma","why":"Pupils confuse the stigma with the anther because both are near the top.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"The ovary","why":"Pupils think the ovary makes pollen because it is at the bottom of the flower.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-t","content":"A bee lands on a flower. Does it fly to the next flower to spread pollen, or stay on this flower to rest?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Fly to the next flower","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Stay and rest","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-q","content":"The bee flies to a new flower. Why is this good for the plant?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"Pollen can reach the stigma of another flower","why":"Cross-pollination mixes pollen between plants, so new seeds can form.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"The bee makes the flower grow taller","why":"Pupils think visits make the plant grow, not that pollen moves.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-c","label":"The bee eats the seeds","why":"Pupils think insects eat seeds, but here the bee only collects nectar.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-q","content":"The bee stays on the same flower. What happens to the pollen?"},{"type":"button","id":"c6-a","label":"It may fall onto the same flower's stigma","why":"Self-pollination can happen, but it gives less variety than pollen from another flower.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"It disappears","why":"Pupils think pollen vanishes if it is not carried away.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c6-c","label":"It turns into a fruit","why":"Pupils mix up pollen with the fruit, which forms after fertilisation.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-term","content":"Seed dispersal"},{"type":"text","id":"c7-def","content":"When seeds are carried away from the parent plant, so they can grow in new places. Wind, water and animals can all do this."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-next","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"Which seed is spread by the wind?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"A dandelion seed with a fluffy parachute","why":"Its light, fluffy shape lets the wind carry it far.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"A heavy conker that falls straight down","why":"Pupils think all seeds are spread the same way, but a heavy seed drops near the parent.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"A seed hidden inside a sticky fruit","why":"Pupils mix up wind dispersal with animal dispersal, which needs a fruit or hooks.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"What do seeds need to start growing?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Water, air and a warm place","why":"A seed needs water, air and suitable warmth to germinate.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Bright light only","why":"Pupils think seeds need light to start, but most seeds start in the dark soil.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Salt and sugar","why":"Pupils think seeds feed on sweets, but they need water and air instead.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which part of the flower becomes the fruit after pollination?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"The ovary","why":"After fertilisation the ovary grows into the fruit that protects the seeds.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"The petals","why":"Pupils think the petals turn into the fruit, but the petals fall off.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"The stamen","why":"Pupils think the stamen becomes the fruit, but it only makes pollen.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"You finished the flower life cycle"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"Well done. You now know how flowers make pollen, how pollen moves, and how seeds travel to new places."}],"actions":[]}}
